Example of `/etc/shadow` Entry

After running the command `grep <username> /etc/shadow`, the output for a user will look something like this:

```
babubutt:$y$j9T$XfhVCvxiuANKLSQgnJz8W.$cS4.vV3WwBoLFPOoWC1lnH7xJco.T9MDOaYwnt4VyeB:20064:0:99999:7::: 
```

Here’s the breakdown of the fields:

1 .babubutt` - The username.
2. $y$j9T$XfhVCvxiuANKLSQgnJz8W... - The encrypted password.
3. 20064 - The last day the password was changed (number of days since January 1, 1970).
4. 0 - The minimum number of days before the user can change the password (0 means no restriction).
5. 99999 - The maximum number of days before the password expires (99999 means the password will never expire unless specified).
6. 7 - The warning period before password expiration in days (7 means the user will be warned 7 days before expiration).
7. :* - Inactive period and account expiration fields, both of which are empty in this example, meaning the account does not have a set expiration or inactivity period.

